557509	1033	1766	ended up packing BoB (my beast of burden) about four hours before my evening flight. I'd already packed up all of my tools and I didn't have a spanner for undoing the pedals. I rushed over to the neighbour's place and borrowed one. Then another problem. BoB has a big frame and didn't quite fit in the box. We pushed the forks through the wall of the box and taped some extra cardboard around the protrusions. I had a rushed shower and glass of champagne and then was dropped at the airport by my long-suffering housemate, who luckily also worships at the altar of chaos.{{joke god name?}}\n\nAll this hurry in Brisbane left me in a tricky position on the other side. I was starting the trip in Munich, Germany - the ending point of a previous trip from Australia to Europe where I'd lived and studied for a couple of years. I also insisted on riding BoB the *entire* way, starting with assembling him at the airport. Which required tools. Since my heroic neighbour had been selfish enough to take back his spanner, I didn't have a way to tighten the pedals. I'd already spent an hour ping-ponging between departments just trying to get rid of my cardboard bike box and wasn't particularly interested in reengaging {{funny metaphor ping pong ball?}}{{german bureaucracy}}. So I screwed them in finger tight. \n\nTheoretically, they're oriented to tighten themselves while riding. Funny how the theories that mean less work are the ones that we embrace. It wasn't the last time that I would pay the price for this kind of motivated wishful thinking. \n\nAnd so, just out of the airport, the crank thread stripped completely and the pedal fell out. I rode the remaining 20km{{real value?}} with my right leg, with my left foot twisted inward awkwardly keeping the left crank spinning.{{Fear about my lack of preparation}}
